<v Ira Glass>Could you sleep? Could Johnny sleep?
<v Ira Glass>你能睡着吗？强尼能睡着吗？

00:40:00.560 --> 00:40:20.840
<v Yang Yi>Well, frankly speaking, I can sleep, and I sleep well. But for Johnny, I think, oh, he's more sensitive during sleeping. So that noisy environment does not work for him. It's obvious he become more and more tired each day.
<v Yang Yi>嗯，坦率地说，我可以睡，我睡得很好。但对于强尼，我想，噢，他在睡觉时更敏感。所以那种吵闹的环境对他不起作用。显然他每天变得越来越累。

00:40:20.840 --> 00:40:25.160
<v Ira Glass>That's not what really gets to Johnny, though. What kills Johnny about all this?
<v Ira Glass>不过，这并不是强尼真正受不了的。到底是什么让强尼崩溃？

00:40:25.160 --> 00:40:42.660
<v Yang Yi>When Johnny realized that there is only one men's toilet. He just-- he broke down. He crashed. [LAUGHS] He just feel, it's such disgusting. It is not healthy.
<v Yang Yi>当强尼意识到只有一个男厕所时。他只是——他崩溃了。他彻底崩了。[笑] 他只是觉得，太恶心了。这不健康。

00:40:42.660 --> 00:40:50.940
<v Ira Glass>He says that to avoid using the bathroom for, I'll just say number two, Johnny took steps, real steps.
<v Ira Glass>他说为了避免使用厕所来解决，我会说，大号，强尼采取了行动，真正的行动。

00:40:50.940 --> 00:41:08.250
<v Yang Yi>Every meal, he just eat half. He just eat half. And the breakfast, the worker give us the bread, eggs, and the milk. He just eat a piece of bread, no eggs, no milk, something like that.
<v Yang Yi>每一顿饭，他只吃一半。他只吃一半。早餐，工作人员给我们面包、鸡蛋和牛奶。他只吃一片面包，没有鸡蛋，没有牛奶，类似那样。

00:41:08.250 --> 00:41:14.220
<v Ira Glass>And is this typical of the two of you? He would get more freaked out about things, and you would be more calm?
<v Ira Glass>你们俩一向如此吗？他会对事情更抓狂，而你会更冷静？

00:41:14.220 --> 00:41:25.650
<v Yang Yi>Well, he is born and raised in Shanghai. It's a big city. And everything is very clean, refined.
<v Yang Yi>嗯，他是在上海出生长大的。这是一个大城市。而且一切都很干净、精致。

00:41:25.650 --> 00:41:26.940
<v Ira Glass>Oh, right.
<v Ira Glass>噢，对。

00:41:26.940 --> 00:41:27.580
<v Yang Yi>Everything's fine.
<v Yang Yi>一切都很好。

00:41:27.580 --> 00:41:29.550
<v Ira Glass>He's used to things being better.
<v Ira Glass>他习惯了更好的东西。

00:41:29.550 --> 00:41:48.570
<v Yang Yi>Yes, yes. So but for me, it's different because I come from a small town. So I have to say, I have seen more dirty toilet than this. So for me, I think it was fine. It would be fine. Because I've seen worse.
<v Yang Yi>是的，是的。所以对我来说，这不同，因为我来自一个小镇。所以我不得不说，我看过比这更脏的厕所。所以对我来说，我觉得还可以。这会好起来的。因为我看过更糟的。

00:41:48.570 --> 00:41:49.410
<v Ira Glass>You've seen worse.
<v Ira Glass>你看过更糟的。

00:41:49.410 --> 00:41:58.100
<v Yang Yi>Yeah, I have seen worse.
<v Yang Yi>是的，我看过更糟的。

00:41:58.100 --> 00:42:11.660
<v Ira Glass>The daily routine at the quarantine center, every other morning at 7:00, everybody would line up at the edge of the pool for a PCR test. When you finish your test, you get breakfast. Young people usually go back to sleep, he says. 7:00 AM is a little early for them.
<v Ira Glass>隔离中心的日常生活，每隔一天的早上7点，大家都会在池边排队进行PCR测试。测试结束时，你得到早餐。年轻人通常会回去睡觉，他说。早上7点对他们来说有点早。

00:42:11.660 --> 00:42:33.980
<v Ira Glass>Pretty soon, their sleep is disturbed by housekeepers who come through spraying disinfectant everywhere, which is really, really loud, he says. He and Johnny would work, do remote meetings with their colleagues. And then when it's almost time for lunch, a nurse stands at the edge of the swimming pool and gets on a megaphone. And in that noisy, echoey room, people talking on their phones--
<v Ira Glass>很快，他们的睡眠就被四处喷洒消毒液的管家打断了，这真的，真的非常吵，他说。他和强尼会工作，与同事进行远程会议。然后当快到午餐时间时，护士站在游泳池边用扩音器喊话。在那嘈杂、回音的房间里，人们在手机上交谈——

00:42:33.980 --> 00:42:44.630
<v Yang Yi>And suddenly, it gets very quiet because everyone knows the nurses are going to read the names of everyone who are allowed to go home.
<v Yang Yi>突然，变得非常安静，因为每个人都知道护士要宣读那些获准回家的人的名字了。

00:42:44.630 --> 00:42:47.460
<v Ira Glass>And what's it like? What's the scene like?
<v Ira Glass>那是什么样的？那是什么场景？

00:42:47.460 --> 00:43:07.100
<v Yang Yi>Well, that scene is very, very funny because the scene is a lot like a reality TV show. You know, those whose names are read cheer. And someone will say, woo! I get to go home! Something like that. And those who are not mentioned have disappointment, reaching all of their faces.
<v Yang Yi>嗯，那个场景非常，非常有趣，因为这个场景很像一个现实电视节目。你知道，那些名字被读到的人会欢呼。有人会说，呜！我可以回家了！类似那样。而那些没被提到的人，失望写满了他们的脸。

00:43:07.100 --> 00:43:14.300
<v Ira Glass>After that, Yi says, everybody tries to figure out, why did that person get out? How many negative tests do you really need to get out?
<v Ira Glass>在那之后，杨说，每个人都在试图弄清楚，为什么那个人能出去？到底需要多少次阴性测试才能出去？

00:43:14.300 --> 00:43:37.970
<v Yang Yi>And there is some gossip is here. Oh, that guy, he already leave, but I don't know. Maybe he's only got one negative, and he's leave. Oh, and that guy. He got two negatives, but his QR code is still red. So that is the main topics in the quarantine center. Everyone will say, oh, how many negatives you get? How many negatives are you get? What's your result? What's your result?
<v Yang Yi>而且这里有一些流言蜚语。噢，那个人，他已经走了，但我不知道。也许他只有一次阴性，他就走了。噢，那个人。他得到两次阴性，但他的二维码仍然是红色的。所以这是隔离中心的主要话题。每个人都会说，噢，你得到多少个阴性？你得到多少个阴性？结果是什么？你的结果是什么？

00:43:37.970 --> 00:43:51.710
<v Ira Glass>And so how does this feel to you and Johnny? You're in this absurd situation. You don't have COVID. But they're keeping you in quarantine. You can't go home. Were you angry? Were you frustrated?
<v Ira Glass>这对你和强尼来说感觉如何？你们处于这种荒谬的情况下。你们没有新冠。但他们把你们关在隔离区。你们不能回家。你们生气吗？你们沮丧吗？

00:43:51.710 --> 00:44:08.930
<v Yang Yi>I think we're not angry and frustrated because-- well, Ira, I have to say, we have no choice. We have no choice. We have to face the reality.
<v Yang Yi>我想我们不生气也不沮丧，因为——嗯，艾拉，我不得不说，我们别无选择。我们别无选择。我们必须面对现实。

00:44:08.930 --> 00:44:16.400
<v Ira Glass>And the quarantine center, he says, wasn't all bad. In fact, a bunch of things were better than their life back home with the city on lockdown.
<v Ira Glass>他说，而且隔离中心并没有那么糟糕。事实上，很多东西比他们在家中处于封锁状态的生活要好。

00:44:16.400 --> 00:44:35.690
<v Yang Yi>I don't have to worry about food anymore. Since the beginning of April, with everything on lockdown, it was hard to find food anywhere. All the stores were closed. Food delivery was completely shut down. And nobody stored enough food. We are hungry.
<v Yang Yi>我不再需要担心食物了。自从四月初开始，一切都处于封锁状态，很难在任何地方找到食物。所有的商店都关门了。食品配送完全关闭。而且没有人储存足够的食物。我们很饿。

00:44:35.690 --> 00:44:37.580
<v Ira Glass>Really? You were hungry?
<v Ira Glass>真的吗？你们很饿？

00:44:37.580 --> 00:44:49.940
<v Yang Yi>Yes, I'm hungry. But here, three meals are served here every day. Pork, beef, eggs, vegetables, and unlimited drinks and milk.
<v Yang Yi>是的，我很饿。但在这里，每天提供三餐。猪肉、牛肉、鸡蛋、蔬菜，以及无限量的饮料和牛奶。

00:44:49.940 --> 00:44:52.140
<v Ira Glass>So that seems really important.
<v Ira Glass>所以那看起来非常重要。

00:44:52.140 --> 00:45:22.340
<v Yang Yi>Yes, and another benefit of being here, we can move around, get some exercise. We're not allowed to leave our homes. And our tiny apartment is only 20 steps at most to walk from one end to the other. But there is a standard swimming pool here to walk around. It is 140 meters. And after each meal, you will see many people walk around the edge of the pool.
<v Yang Yi>是的，来到这里的另一个好处是，我们可以四处走动，做些运动。我们不被允许离开我们的家。而我们的小公寓走动从一端到另一端最多只有20步。但这里有一个标准游泳池可以走动。它有140米。而在每一顿饭后，你会看到很多人在池边走动。

00:45:22.340 --> 00:45:36.020
<v Ira Glass>This is another thing. Back at home, Yi says, at their apartment building, people would only see each other when they went downstairs for the official COVID test. When they did that, everybody was pretty quick about it. Nobody would stay and chat, scared of getting infected.
<v Ira Glass>这是另一件事。回到家，杨说，在他们的公寓大楼里，人们只有在下楼进行官方新冠测试时才会见面。当他们这样做时，每个人都很快。没有人会留下来聊天，害怕被感染。

00:45:36.020 --> 00:45:39.710
<v Yang Yi>But here, everyone is infected.
<v Yang Yi>但在这里，每个人都被感染了。

00:45:39.710 --> 00:45:49.400
<v Ira Glass>Well, everybody but them. And they were all stuck together, so people talked and hung out. And you can just watch people, which he likes.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，除了他们以外的所有人。而且他们都被困在一起，所以人们交谈和闲逛。而且你可以看着人们，这让他喜欢。

00:45:49.400 --> 00:45:56.410
<v Yang Yi>Yeah, I hadn't seen so many strangers in weeks! And what impressed me the most was a young couple.
<v Yang Yi>是的，我已经好几周没见过这么多陌生人了！而最让我印象深刻的是一对年轻夫妇。

00:45:56.410 --> 00:45:59.630
<v Yang Yi>[GIGGLING] [SPEAKING CHINESE]
<v Yang Yi>[咯咯笑] [讲中文]

00:45:59.630 --> 00:46:06.590
<v Yang Yi>This is me saying, they seem to be sunbathing. It's like, they're laying on the beach.
<v Yang Yi>这是我说，他们看起来好像在日光浴。就像，他们躺在沙滩上。

00:46:06.590 --> 00:46:09.260
<v Ira Glass>Wait, because why? Explain their setup.
<v Ira Glass>等等，因为为什么？解释一下他们的设置。

00:46:09.260 --> 00:46:22.500
<v Yang Yi>Oh, because they moved their cot to the side of the pool. And for them, so they were like the beach chairs, you know. And each laying there by the pool with a book in their hands.
<v Yang Yi>噢，因为他们把帆布床搬到了池边。对他们来说，就像海滩椅，你知道的。每个人都躺在那里，手里拿着一本书。

00:46:22.500 --> 00:46:25.640
<v Ira Glass>Honestly, when you describe it this way, it doesn't sound so bad.
<v Ira Glass>老实说，当你这样描述时，听起来并不那么糟糕。

00:46:25.640 --> 00:46:32.200
<v Yang Yi>Yeah, I feel that here in quarantine center, normal life is lived.
<v Yang Yi>是的，我觉得在这里隔离中心，生活还在继续。

00:46:32.200 --> 00:46:55.630
<v Ira Glass>Other people Yi observed-- a middle-aged businessman, big guy, big voice, very pro-government. He would spout off after breakfast every day about politics at length, for everyone around him, like his own political talk show. On the cot next to him, a skinny university lecturer who Yi saw try to argue with the businessman one day, which, apparently, did not go very well. The business man just kind of drowned him out.
<v Ira Glass>杨观察到的其他人——一个中年商人，大块头，大嗓门，非常支持政府。他每天早餐后都会就政治问题长篇大论，对周围的每个人讲，就像他自己的政治脱口秀一样。在他旁边的帆布床上，坐着一位瘦弱的大学讲师，杨曾有一天看到他试图与商人争论，这显然进展得不太顺利。商人只是用声音淹没了他的争论。

00:46:55.630 --> 00:47:11.350
<v Ira Glass>And then there was the guy who slept on the cot next to Yi. He was a source of fascination because, more than anybody in the quarantine center, he seemed the most desperate to get out, the most upset each day when he was not on the list to go home. Yi didn't know his first name.
<v Ira Glass>还有睡在杨旁边帆布床上的那个人。他令人着迷，因为他似乎比隔离中心里的任何人都更渴望出去，每天当他不在回家名单上时就越沮丧。杨不知道他的名字。

00:47:11.350 --> 00:47:23.860
<v Yang Yi>I usually call him Wang. Clean cut, 26, worked as an English teacher in a kindergarten. And before I came to the quarantine center, he's already here for one week.
<v Yang Yi>我通常叫他王。清爽，26岁，在幼儿园担任英语老师。在我来到隔离中心之前，他已经在这里待了一周了。

00:47:23.860 --> 00:47:24.640
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:47:24.640 --> 00:47:34.090
<v Yang Yi>And the day after I arrived, he had already gotten two negative test results, but they didn't let him leave.
<v Yang Yi>而在我到达后的第二天，他已经得到了两次阴性测试结果，但他们不让他离开。

00:47:34.090 --> 00:47:37.210
<v Wang>[SPEAKING CHINESE]
<v Wang>[讲中文]

00:47:37.210 --> 00:47:41.650
<v Yang Yi>That's Wang's voice, and he's saying he was getting desperate.
<v Yang Yi>那是王的声音，他说他变得绝望了。

00:47:41.650 --> 00:47:54.160
<v Ira Glass>One of the ways that Wang deals with his anxiety over this, he never changes his clothes. He says that he's in the same red shirt and white socks every single day, even though he has a big suitcase by his cot.
<v Ira Glass>王应对这种焦虑的一种方式是，他从不换衣服。他说他每天都穿同一件红衬衫和白袜子，尽管他的帆布床旁有一个大行李箱。

00:47:54.160 --> 00:48:23.950
<v Yang Yi>But he never opened that suitcase. And I ask him why. He tells me, he just wants to go home. He just wants to go home when the nurse read his name, and leave immediately and pack the suitcase. Maybe it will spend five or eight or 10 minutes. He don't want to waste up time. So he think, OK, maybe I don't open that suitcase.
<v Yang Yi>但他从来没打开过那个行李箱。我问他为什么。他告诉我，他只是想回家。他只是想在护士读到他的名字时，立即离开并收拾行李箱。也许它会花费五到八或十分钟。他不想浪费时间。所以他想，好吧，也许我不打开那个行李箱。

00:48:23.950 --> 00:48:29.860
<v Ira Glass>Some nights, Wang stays up all night, worried that he's going to sleep through the PCR test at 7:00 AM.
<v Ira Glass>有些晚上，王整夜不睡，担心自己会睡过早上7点的PCR测试。

00:48:29.860 --> 00:48:33.580
<v Wang>[SPEAKING CHINESE]
<v Wang>[讲中文]

00:48:33.580 --> 00:48:47.670
<v Yang Yi>Here's Wang's voice, and he's saying, "I can't sleep all night. I was waiting. My mind was thinking all the time, go home, go home, go home. I was not even in the mood to watch TikTok anymore."
<v Yang Yi>这是王的声音，他说，“我整晚睡不着。我在等待。我的大脑一直在想，回家，回家，回家。我甚至连刷抖音的心情都没有了。”

00:48:47.670 --> 00:49:25.280
<v Ira Glass>Finally, one morning, they read Wang's name. Yi says he was out of there in three minutes flat. Threw his sleeping bag and toiletries into the garbage, pulled his unopened suitcase out of there before Yi could get his phone number or say goodbye. Johnny and Yi got out the next day. They got the dog back. QR codes were fixed. They had started in their apartment, went on a six-day journey that was completely pointless, and delivered them back at the end of the road to the exact spot they had started, no better and no worse, though Yi says he's glad it happened.
<v Ira Glass>终于，一天早上，他们读到了王的名字。杨说他在三分钟内就离开了那里。把睡袋和洗漱用品扔进垃圾桶，在他能拿到电话号码或说再见之前就拉着他没打开的行李箱出去了。强尼和杨在第二天出去了。他们找回了狗。二维码修好了。他们从公寓出发，进行了一次完全毫无意义的六天旅程，最后回到路终点，恰好回到他们出发的原点，没有更好也没有更坏，尽管杨说他很高兴这件事发生了。

00:49:25.280 --> 00:49:47.250
<v Yang Yi>I have to say, it feel like an adventure. It's just like an adventure. We got food. We could talk to the strangers. And that is the only chance during those weeks we could go outside apartment. We could go on the street.
<v Yang Yi>我不得不说，感觉就像一场冒险。它就像一场冒险。我们有食物。我们可以和陌生人交谈。那是那些周里我们可以走出公寓的唯一机会。我们可以走在大街上。

00:49:47.250 --> 00:50:29.270
<v Ira Glass>When they're dropped off afterwards, it was at a crossroads near their place. And they walked through the empty streets to get home. Yi liked that. No cars, no people. Just cats, he says, just strolling in the middle of the street like they own the place. When would you ever see that? Or any of it? If he had a choice, Yi definitely would choose the experience they had. Johnny, however? No way.
<v Ira Glass>当他们事后被送下车时，是在他们住处附近的一个十字路口。他们走过空荡荡的街道回家。杨喜欢那样。没有车，没有行人。只有猫，他说，像它们拥有这个地方一样在街道中间溜达。你什么时候会看到那样的情况？或者任何那样的情况？如果有的选择，杨肯定会选择他们拥有的那次经历。然而强尼？没门。

00:50:29.270 --> 00:50:44.480
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, "The Roe Less Traveled." So ladies and gentlemen, perhaps you remember the critique offered by a 10-year-old at the beginning of today's program about the way we spell the name of a certain fish, S-A-L-M-O-N.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“少有人走的路”。女士们先生们，或许你们还记得今天节目开始时一位10岁小朋友对我们拼写某种鱼名字的方式所提出的批评，S-A-L-M-O-N。

00:50:44.480 --> 00:50:51.260
<v Cassidy>It would be pronounced sal-mon, but it's salmon. Like, why?
<v Cassidy>按理说应该读作sal-mon，但实际上读作sam-on。就像，为什么？

00:50:51.260 --> 00:51:08.510
<v Ira Glass>Fair point. This last story is about salmon doing something about as logical as that spelling, taking a ridiculous route to get where they need to go. And by that, I mean a ridiculous route that is different from the normal kind of ridiculous route they usually take. Jaime Lowe went to California to see it for herself.
<v Ira Glass>公平的观点。最后一个故事是关于鲑鱼做的事情，逻辑上和那个拼写一样荒谬，走着一条荒谬的路线到达它们需要去的地方。我的意思是一条荒谬的路线，与它们通常走的那些荒谬路线不同。杰米·洛亲自去加州一探究竟。

00:51:08.510 --> 00:51:24.950
<v Jaime Lowe>I have an above-average attachment to salmon, an obsession that began in college when I took a class with salmon guru Peter Moyle. He's famous in salmon circles, trust me. I think I was attracted to their epic migration and the macabre nature of their lives.
<v Jaime Lowe>我对鲑鱼有着超乎寻常的依恋，这种痴迷始于我在大学时参加了鲑鱼大师彼得·莫伊尔的课程。他，相信我，在鲑鱼圈子里很有名。我想我是被它们史诗般的迁徙和它们生命中那种恐怖的本质所吸引。

00:51:24.950 --> 00:51:54.050
<v Jaime Lowe>They're born, wiggle around in a cold stream, make their way to the ocean, then instinctually, against all odds, return to the place of their birth, hurling their bodies over waterfalls, flesh rotting, dodging bear claws, simultaneously decaying as they're pushing towards their exact nook and their exact birth stream to lay eggs and let the cycle begin again. It's like they're dying to live.
<v Jaime Lowe>它们出生，在寒冷的溪流中扭动，设法到达海洋，然后凭借本能，克服一切困难，回到它们的出生地，将身体投向瀑布，肉体腐烂，躲避熊爪，同时在向它们确切的出生溪流推行，去产卵并让周期再次开始。就好像它们拼死也要活下去。

00:51:54.050 --> 00:52:07.910
<v Jaime Lowe>I mean, there's this whole industry of nature documentaries centered on dramatizing the species and their odyssey, films which feature the sound of raging water in the background while a serious narrator describes their majesty.
<v Jaime Lowe>我的意思是，有一整套以戏剧化这一物种及其奥德赛为中心的自然纪录片行业，这些影片以背景中湍急的水声为特色，同时一位严肃的叙述者描述它们的威严。

00:52:07.910 --> 00:52:18.490
<v Narrator>It's a game of persistence and luck. And although they fail time after time, their desire to push on is so strong, they never give up.
<v Narrator>这是一场关于坚持和运气的游戏。虽然它们一次又一次地失败，但它们坚持推进的愿望是如此强烈，它们从不放弃。

00:52:18.490 --> 00:52:50.740
<v Jaime Lowe>It's a staple of nature porn. But the world has changed. The climate has changed. There's been increasingly hot temperatures, fires, drought, pollution. And many of the rivers salmon used for their migration have run dry or have been blocked by dams. That's why today, in California, many salmon follow a radically different course. They make their way to the ocean not through rivers, but inside a fleet of tanker trucks with human drivers at the wheel.
<v Jaime Lowe>这是自然色情片的主打内容。但世界变了。气候变了。气温越来越高，火灾，干旱，污染。许多鲑鱼用于迁徙的河流已经干涸或被大坝阻断。这就是为什么今天在加州，许多鲑鱼遵循着完全不同的路线。它们不是通过河流，而是乘坐有人类司机驾驶的油罐车舰队前往海洋。

00:52:50.740 --> 00:53:31.920
<v Jaime Lowe>When I first heard about trucking salmon, it seemed so dystopic, a cold, mechanical process. The idea of salmon stuck in traffic just made me feel sad, like nature had been hijacked. But I was also kind of curious. I really wanted to see this Rube Goldberg, tin-cans-and-rubber-bands environmental hack in person. So I decided to follow five silver trucks transporting about half a million adolescent salmon on a 120-mile drive to the San Francisco Bay, a migration that no longer starts with the sound of babbling water. It's got a different sound.
<v Jaime Lowe>当我第一次听说用卡车运输鲑鱼时，它看起来是那么反乌托邦，一个寒冷、机械的过程。鲑鱼被困在交通中的想法让我感到悲伤，好像自然被劫持了。但我也有点好奇。我真的很想亲眼看看这个鲁布·戈德堡式的、用锡罐和橡皮筋拼凑的环保黑客手段。所以我决定跟随五辆银色的卡车，运送大约50万条青少年鲑鱼，进行120英里的车程前往旧金山湾，一场不再以潺潺水声开始的迁徙。它有一种不同的声音。

00:53:31.920 --> 00:53:37.380
<v Jaime Lowe>[MACHINERY HUMMING]
<v Jaime Lowe>[机器嗡嗡声]

00:53:37.380 --> 00:54:14.790
<v Jaime Lowe>Late one Tuesday afternoon, I found myself at the Mokelumne River Fish Hatchery, standing in front of a gas-powered machine that looked like it would be more at home on a construction site. The hatchery is about an hour northeast of Modesto in the Central Valley. It's rural and isolated, and so hot, the sun immediately burns, even in May. Mokelumne sits next to a river that was once known for thriving salmon and trout runs, but was dammed in the '60s. To offset the lost wild populations, the state built a hatchery and a parking lot close to the old stream bed.
<v Jaime Lowe>周二傍晚，我发现自己站在莫凯勒米河鱼类孵化场前，站在一台看起来更适合施工现场的燃气机器前。孵化场位于中央谷地莫德斯托东北约一小时车程的地方。那里农村且孤立，而且太热，即使在五月，阳光也会立刻灼伤皮肤。莫凯勒米坐落在一条曾经以繁茂的鲑鱼和鳟鱼洄游而闻名的河流旁，但在60年代被筑坝了。为了抵消失去的野生种群，该州在旧溪床附近建造了一个孵化场和一个停车场。

00:54:14.790 --> 00:54:45.810
<v Jaime Lowe>I should say, hatcheries are controversial. The fish raised in them are not as prepared to survive in nature. And when they're released, they breed with native salmon, compromising the gene pool. There are people who think we shouldn't have hatcheries at all. But California has had them for decades at this point, a really long time. Mokelumne is not a massive operation, just a few bungalows and 20 concrete trenches packed with adolescent fish.
<v Jaime Lowe>我得说，孵化场是有争议的。在其中饲养的鱼并没有做好在自然界生存的准备。当它们被释放时，它们会与本地鲑鱼杂交，损害基因库。有些人认为我们根本不应该有孵化场。但加州在这一点上已经持续了几十年，真的很长一段时间。莫凯勒米不是一个大型作业，只有几间平房和20个挤满了青少年鱼类的混凝土沟渠。

00:54:45.810 --> 00:55:01.260
<v Jaime Lowe>When I pull up, I meet the guy responsible for getting the salmon to the ocean. Bill Smith, the hatchery manager, greets me, then walks me over to this machine that's now so critical to salmon migration, and we try to talk over its roar.
<v Jaime Lowe>当我停下车时，我遇到了负责把鲑鱼送到海洋的那个人。孵化场经理比尔·史密斯向我打招呼，然后带我走向这台现在对鲑鱼迁徙至关重要的机器，我们试着在机器的轰鸣声中交谈。

00:55:01.260 --> 00:55:03.150
<v Jaime Lowe>What is this?
<v Jaime Lowe>这是什么？

00:55:03.150 --> 00:55:04.540
<v Bill Smith>This is a fish crowder.
<v Bill Smith>这是一个鱼类挤压器。

00:55:04.540 --> 00:55:05.680
<v Jaime Lowe>A fish crowder?
<v Jaime Lowe>鱼类挤压器？

00:55:05.680 --> 00:55:09.550
<v Bill Smith>Yeah. It's what we use to crowd the fish down.
<v Bill Smith>是的。这是我们用来把鱼群挤过去的。

00:55:09.550 --> 00:55:11.010
<v Jaime Lowe>Oh, I see them!
<v Jaime Lowe>噢，我看到它们了！

00:55:11.010 --> 00:55:12.150
<v Bill Smith>See all the salmon?
<v Bill Smith>看见所有的鲑鱼了吗？

00:55:12.150 --> 00:55:12.530
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah!
<v Jaime Lowe>是的！

00:55:12.530 --> 00:55:13.780
<v Bill Smith>Yeah.
<v Bill Smith>是的。

00:55:13.780 --> 00:55:48.340
<v Jaime Lowe>Bill's a big, bearded, burly guy. And the suspenders are not just for show. They hold up jeans which are tucked into waterproof boots. He's introverted, seems a little gruff. But there's a tenderness in the way he talks about the fish. In fact, he tells me, when these fish were just born, babies the size of fingernails, wriggling in the water, he would sometimes sleep at the hatchery overnight. He wanted to make sure their water was the right temperature, that the electricity stayed on. He wanted to make sure they were OK.
<v Jaime Lowe>是的。

00:55:48.340 --> 00:55:54.430
<v Jaime Lowe>Do you feel emotional attachment to the fish after, because you basically--
<v Jaime Lowe>比尔是一个高大、多须、魁梧的男人。背带不仅仅是装饰。它们支撑着塞进防水靴里的牛仔裤。他很内向，看起来有点粗鲁。但在他谈论鱼的方式中有一种温柔。事实上，他告诉我，当这些鱼刚出生时，只有指甲盖那么大的婴儿，在水中扭动，他有时会整晚睡在孵化场。他想确保它们的水温合适，确保电力没断。他想确保它们没事。

00:55:54.430 --> 00:56:00.410
<v Bill Smith>I don't know if you would call it that, but yeah, definitely an attachment. Pretty amazing animals.
<v Bill Smith>你对比这些鱼产生过情感依恋吗，因为你基本上——

00:56:00.410 --> 00:56:01.700
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.
<v Jaime Lowe>我不知道你是否会那样称呼它，但是的，绝对有依恋。非常了不起的动物。

00:56:01.700 --> 00:56:22.490
<v Ira Glass>It's comforting to know someone who cares is at the heart of this operation. Bill grew up in the area, a fisherman. His life revolves around raising these animals for harvest. But he rarely buys salmon in the store, says he can't afford it. He only eats what he catches.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:56:22.490 --> 00:56:55.820
<v Ira Glass>He mounted the moving platform of the crowder and started in on his job. The crowder has a metal grate that dips below the water. It pushes the fish, each the size of a hot dog, to one end of the raceway. Then they're sucked up into a tube that's connected to the top of a truck. The tube was translucent. And I could see all these salmon whooshing by, like documents being transported in a pneumatic tube.
<v Ira Glass>知道有这么一个关心它们的人处于这项运作的核心，让人感到安慰。比尔在该地区长大，是个渔夫。他的生活围绕着饲养这些动物以供捕获。但他很少在商店里买鲑鱼，说他买不起。他只吃他捕获的。

00:56:55.820 --> 00:57:23.240
<v Ira Glass>I wondered how they felt, if they felt. What did they think of the sudden rush of movement, the dramatic shift in their environment? One minute, shining in the sun, the next, dropped in a big, dark tanker. There was no way to really know. After Bill filled each truck with brackish water and fish, and bags of ice to maintain the temperature, we pulled out of the hatchery.
<v Ira Glass>他登上了挤压器的移动平台，开始了他的工作。挤压器有一个金属格栅，浸没在水下。它把每一条热狗大小的鱼推到水道的一端。然后它们被吸进连接在卡车顶部的一根管子里。管子是半透明的。我可以看到所有这些鲑鱼像在气动管中运输的文件一样呼啸而过。

00:57:23.240 --> 00:57:26.810
<v Jaime Lowe>Goodbye, fishies.
<v Jaime Lowe>我很好奇它们的感觉，它们是否会有感觉。它们对突如其来的运动、环境中剧烈的变化有什么看法？一分钟前，在阳光下闪耀，下一分钟，被扔进一个巨大的、黑暗的油罐里。真的没法知道。在比尔用咸水、鱼和几袋冰来维持温度装满每辆卡车后，我们驶离了孵化场。

00:57:26.810 --> 00:57:48.870
<v Ira Glass>We're on the road, me and 500,000 fish, and it's weird. Bill's driving ahead in a pickup. I follow behind the caravan. The five trucks look like any other fuel tankers you would see on the highway. They blend in seamlessly. You'd never know how many lives were speeding by in the lane next to you.
<v Ira Glass>再见，小鱼们。

00:57:48.870 --> 00:58:18.490
<v Ira Glass>What's amazing is that even though the fish are traveling by truck, they'll still be able to trace their way back to their birthplace, the hatchery. The fish are imprinted with hatchery water, Bill told me. So if they survive long enough at sea to reach spawning age, they can use their sense of smell to return to him, swimming up rivers till they're at the stream bed by the hatchery.
<v Ira Glass>我们在路上，我和50万条鱼，这很奇怪。比尔开着一辆皮卡车在前面。我跟在车队后面。这五辆卡车看起来和你会在高速公路上看到的任何其他油罐车一样。它们天衣无缝地融合在一起。你永远不会知道有多少生命在你旁边的车道上飞驰而过。

00:58:18.490 --> 00:58:50.440
<v Ira Glass>It takes 2 and 1/2 hours till the trucks finally pull in to their destination, Fort Baker. Nothing can happen until the dead of night because if the salmon are released when it's light out, seagulls, pelicans, and cormorants will circle, dive, and swallow as many of the 500,000 fish as they can. In front of us, the Golden Gate Bridge frames the San Francisco Bay, which glimmers against the skyline. They can't get very close to the water because the pier is a historic landmark.
<v Ira Glass>令人惊奇的是，尽管这些鱼是通过卡车运输的，但它们仍然能够循迹回到它们的出生地——孵化场。比尔告诉我，这些鱼身上烙印着孵化场水的记忆。所以如果它们在海上生存足够久达到产卵年龄，它们可以使用嗅觉回到他那里，逆流而上直到它们来到孵化场旁边的溪床。

00:58:50.440 --> 00:59:33.190
<v Ira Glass>So Bill sets up a chute, which extends about 100 feet. It connects the truck to the edge of the pier. Then there's a 20-foot drop from the pier to the bay. Darkness finally closes in, and Bill signals that it's time. As oblivious teenagers make out at the end of the pier, a slow trickle of water drips down into the bay. Then a steady stream flows with a few of the small fish. Then a cascade, a rush all at once, crowded and alive. The salmon are falling in a flurry to the bay below, crashing against the water, then making small waves of their own.
<v Ira Glass>两小时三十分钟后，卡车终于到达了目的地，贝克堡。在深夜之前什么都做不了，因为如果鲑鱼在光天化日之下被释放，海鸥、鹈鹕和鸬鹚就会盘旋、俯冲，吞下它们能吞下的那50万条鱼中的尽可能多。在我们面前，金门大桥勾勒出了旧金山湾，在天际线下闪烁。它们不能离水太近，因为码头是一个历史地标。

00:59:33.190 --> 01:00:23.990
<v Ira Glass>It's chaotic and beautiful, but everything about this is fucked up. It feels crude and kind of rude to the animals. I feel like we don't even deserve salmon, if this is the world we've made. But at the same time-- and I hate that I'm saying this-- it's not so bad. I love salmon, and I want them to survive. And if we're going to have salmon in California, this might be the only way. Bill repeats the process with each truck four more times. A few fish flop on the pier, and he picks them up tenderly and throws them in the water with the others. He's gotten them as far as he can.
<v Ira Glass>所以比尔架起了一个大约100英尺长的滑槽。它连接卡车和码头边缘。然后从码头到海湾有20英尺的落差。黑暗终于笼罩了，比尔示意时间到了。当不知情的青少年在码头尽头亲热时，一滴水珠慢慢滴进海湾。然后一股稳定的水流带着几条小鱼流下。然后是一道瀑布，一股瞬间爆发的急流，拥挤而鲜活。鲑鱼在阵风中跌落到下面的海湾，撞击着水面，然后掀起属于它们自己的小波浪。

01:00:23.990 --> 01:00:31.650
<v Ira Glass>Jaime Lowe, her most recent book is called Breathing Fire, Female Inmate Firefighters on the Front Lines of California's Wildfires.
<v Ira Glass>这很混乱，也很美丽，但关于这所有的一切都是操蛋的。它感觉很粗糙，而且对动物有点粗鲁。我觉得如果我们创造了这样的世界，我们甚至不配拥有鲑鱼。但与此同时——我讨厌我说这话——情况并没有那么糟糕。我爱鲑鱼，我想让它们生存。如果我们要在加州拥有鲑鱼，这可能是唯一的方法。比尔又重复了四次每辆卡车的过程。几条鱼在码头上扑腾，他温柔地把它们捡起来，扔进水里和其他鱼在一起。他已经把它们送到他所能送到的最远的地方了。

